<h1 class="entry-title">Cu Chi Tunnels</h1>
</header>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="page-spacer clearfix">
<div class="content-area" id="primary">
<div class="container">
<div class="row">
<main class="site-main col-xs-12 col-sm-8 left-block" id="main">
<article class="post hentry">
<a href="/en/blog-details.html">
</a>
<header class="entry-header">
</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<div class="entry-content">
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/ddcc5.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Source: hanoimoi.vn</div>
</div>
<h3>Introduction Article</h3>
<h4>• Historical Development:</h4>
<p>
    - Cu Chi Tunnels began to be built during the resistance war against the French colonialists. When newly built, tunnels were just short shelters with a simple structure for soldiers to hide and hide documents. But the secret bunker has the disadvantage that when discovered, it is easy to be captured or destroyed by the enemy. Since then it has been thought that it is necessary to extend the secret cellar into tunnels and open up the surface with many secret doors to both hide and fight the enemy, and when necessary, to escape the danger to a distance to other places.<br/>
- Entering the period against the American imperialists invading Vietnam, the Cu Chi tunnels flourished, especially in early 1966. The movement of tunneling is growing rapidly and strongly everywhere, young and old, boys and girls eager to participate in the construction of tunnels to fight the enemy. Man's willpower won the difficulty. With only very rudimentary tools such as hoes and bamboo shovels, Cu Chi soldiers and people created a massive work with hundreds of kilometers of underground tunnels, connecting hamlets and villages together as a magical "underground village". The tunnel system is 250 kilometres long, with 3 different depths, the upper layer is about 3 metres from the ground, the middle layer is about 5 to 8 metres from the ground, and the bottom layer is more than 12 metres deep.<br/>
- During the years of resistance, the US Army continuously attacked to destroy the base area and tunnel system. They used tricks such as: Pumping water into the tunnels, using an army of "rats" to destroy them, using herring dogs to detect and destroy them, using motor vehicles to bulldoze, etc. But officials and the people of Cu Chi district still resisted so that these plans never succeeded.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/ddcc2.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Source: saigonstarttravel.com</div>
</div>
<h4>• Structure:</h4>
<p>
    - Tunnel architecture is inherited and valuable in many aspects, especially in terms of military art and people's war, with outstanding creativity, which developed to its peak during the resistance period Anti-American, which the whole world must recognize.
- Cu Chi Tunnels is a system of tunnels running zigzag underground, from the "backbone" (main road) radiating countless long and short branches, connecting with each other, or dotted independently depending on the terrain. . There are many branches branching out into the Saigon River so that when faced with a critical situation, you can cross the river to another base area.<br/>
- The tunnel is dug on an area of clay mixed with laterite so it is highly durable and less susceptible to landslides. The tunnel system is capable of resisting artillery shells and the weight of tanks and armored vehicles, and the deep tunnel sections can withstand small-sized bombs. There are structural sections with 2 to 3 floors (the upper floor is called “upper floor,” the lower floor is called “lower floor”). The up and down space between floors has a secret hatch.<br/>
- In the tunnels, there are stoppers at necessary points to prevent enemies or toxic chemicals sprayed by the enemy. There are narrow sections (waist), you have to dodge to get through. Along the tunnel there are vents above, discreetly camouflaged and carved out of the ground with many secret doors. Many doors are structured into combat bays, sniper emplacement is very flexible. Under the tunnels in the dangerous areas, there are tunnels, nail holes, pitfalls...<br/>
- Around the entrance and exit of the tunnel, there are many spike tunnels, nail pits, left mines (called death zones), including large anti-tank mines and anti-helicopter grenade launchers, in order to destroy and prevent troops. The enemy approaches.<br/>
- Continuity with tunnels with wide tunnels that can be hooked up to a hammock to rest. In the bunker, there is a place to store weapons, food, and food, a water well, a "Hoang Cam" kitchen, a leader's working tunnel, a surgical tunnel, and a nurturing tunnel for wounded soldiers, a solid A-shaped tunnel for women, the elderly and children take shelter. There are large tunnels, cool roofs, cleverly camouflaged above for meetings, cultural performances...
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/ddcc3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Source: iwater.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Life in the tunnels</h4>
<p>
    Living and fighting conditions in the tunnels were extremely difficult. Because it was necessary to preserve forces to fight, the soldiers and people of Cu Chi had to accept all the harshness that could be said to be beyond human endurance. Only being able to crawl or stoop when moving, lack of air to breathe, lack of light, humidity and heat, insects, snakes and centipedes... so almost the majority of people living in the tunnels suffer from parasites dermatology and bone diseases. In addition, the lack of food and necessities is also the biggest problem for tunnel residents. It can be said that life in such tunnels is the pinnacle of patriotism, endurance, patience and desire for peace of the people of Cu Chi. Just as a foreign politician said when going down to visit the tunnels: "For many years I have been skeptical about the struggle of the Vietnamese people. How can a small and poor country defeat a large and rich country like America? But when I came here, going through the 70m tunnel, I was able to answer that question myself."
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/ddcc4.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Source: vietnamnet.vn</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</article>
